MVC (Model View Controller)
--> It is a design pattern used to organize an application into three separate parts:

Model → Stores data and business logic.
View → Displays data to the user.
Controller → Receives user requests and connects Model and View.

--> MVC helps keep code clean, organized, and easy to maintain.
| Part       | Responsibility        |
| ---------- | --------------------- |
| Model      | Data + Business Logic |
| View       | User Interface        |
| Controller | Request Handling      |

Internal Working :
Suppose user opens : http://localhost:8080/student

Step 1
Request goes to Controller.
//StudentController
Step 2
Controller asks Model for data.
//StudentService
Step 3
Model fetches data.
//Student Name = Teju
Step 4
Data returned to Controller.
Step 5
Controller sends data to View.
Step 6
View displays data.
//Welcome Teju

Spring MVC Code Example :

Model -->
package com.example.model;

public class Student 
{
    private String name;

    public Student(String name) 
    {
        this.name = name;
    }
    public String getName() 
    {
        return name;
    }
}
//private String name; Stores student name.
//this.name = name; Stores value inside variable.
//public String getName() --> Getter method, Returns student name.

Controller -->
package com.example.controller;

import com.example.model.Student;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Controller;
import org.springframework.ui.Model;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;

@Controller
public class StudentController 
{
    @GetMapping("/student")
    public String showStudent(Model model) 
    {
        Student student = new Student("Teju");
        model.addAttribute("student", student);
        return "student";
    }
}

//@GetMapping("/student") --> Handles URL : /student
When user visits : localhost:8080/student,this method executes.
//public String getStudent(Model model) --> Method executed when request arrives.

View -->
(student.html)
